---
import Film from '../../plate/Film.astro';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './style.css';

const formula = (tex: string) => renderFormula(tex, false);

const labels = [
  { id: 'caustic-equation', tex: String.raw`\frac{x^2}{1.96-\lambda}+\frac{y^2}{1-\lambda}=1` },
  { id: 'wobble-equation', tex: String.raw`(1+0.01\cos3t)(1.4\cos t,\ \sin t)` },
];
---

<Film
  tag="uruk-caustic-collar"
  cues={CUES}
  timeline={TIMELINE}
  description="A film of reflected billiard paths in an ellipse and a one-percent nonelliptical deformation, alongside their computed phase portraits. Nested caustics, earlier results and periodic polygon families explain the unreviewed claim that a continuous gap-free band near the rim forces an ellipse. A complete text transcript follows."
  caption="Billiards in an ellipse and in the same table changed by one percent: caustics and the phase cylinder, what earlier theorems assumed, the claim that a gap-free band of caustics by the rim forces an ellipse, and the broken three-bounce curve of periodic orbits."
>
  <Fragment slot="labels">
    {labels.map((label) => <span class="film-label film-label--equation" data-label={label.id}><span set:html={formula(label.tex)} /></span>)}
  </Fragment>
  <p class="film-method">How the film is made. The table is (1.4 cos t, sin t), or that same boundary multiplied by 1 + 0.01 cos 3t. Impacts are found by root-finding on the boundary, with the outgoing ray reflected in its computed normal. Arclength is integrated numerically. The ellipse's foci are about ±0.979796. Its perimeter is about 7.592274; the wobble's is about 7.593965. Both tables have positive curvature. The animation gives each flight explanatory time; it illustrates the exact reflection geometry rather than a physical clock.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The three ellipse launches start at t = 0.3 with outgoing angles 0.45, 0.25 and 0.12 radians from the counterclockwise tangent. Their confocal parameters are about 0.205057, 0.066340 and 0.015533. Each segment is tangent to the corresponding caustic. Shallower launches put that caustic closer to the rim. Only the ellipse has the exact confocal caustics drawn here. A collar marked on the wobble illustrates an assumed hypothesis and is labelled schematic.</p>
  <p class="film-method">Each phase portrait uses 44 launches, with initial angles from 0.04 to 1.55 and varying starting points, followed for 700 impacts. A pixel records arclength divided by perimeter and the outgoing angle. The full strip displays angles from zero to π. It also plots the exact time-reversed impacts, replacing δ by π − δ; these are the same billiard geometry traversed in reverse. During the single-shot replay, phase dots correspond only to the actual forward impacts and arrive with the ball.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The ellipse's upper phase ovals do not go around the cylinder. They correspond to confocal hyperbolic caustics near the short-axis two-bounce orbit. They are different from the essential invariant curves that wrap around the cylinder near grazing. Bialy's whole-strip hypothesis requires the latter kind throughout the phase space, which the ellipse's nonwrapping ovals do not satisfy. The label integrable includes the ellipse's invariant levels, not a claim that every level is a closed convex caustic around the table.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The wobble portraits are computed numerical demonstrations. The near-rim gaps are too small to resolve at this scale, and white space between finitely sampled traces does not measure those gaps. Islands and a spreading orbit higher up illustrate the change in dynamics. Neither a finite portrait nor this small deformation supplies a proof of a gap-free collar or its absence.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The closed three-, four- and five-sided polygons are optimized at 96 starting points per table. The display interpolates their boundary parameters and polishes the free vertices, keeping every vertex on the rim. On the ellipse the lengths are about 6.400095, 6.881860 and 7.128145 for every start, and the polygons are periodic billiard orbits. On the wobble the three-sided length ranges from about 6.350516 to 6.452051. At a general fixed start the optimized closed polygon need not obey reflection at that pinned start; periodic billiard orbits occur only at stationary starts of its length function. These low-order examples illustrate the rational-family idea; they need not lie in an arbitrarily thin grazing collar and do not themselves prove the claimed theorem.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The ledger's shaded strips are schematic hypotheses, separating gaps near the rim, Bialy's whole-strip condition, closeness to an ellipse and the claimed continuous gap-free collar. Individual physical caustics are smooth; dependence from one leaf to another need only be continuous. The companion starts from continuous essential invariant curves and constructs the physical collar. The complex continuation and its case list are schematic. The full Birkhoff conjecture with gaps is a separate statement. The ending follows the article's unreviewed label, and no Lean statement exists for this family.</p>
</Film>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
